---------------------------- MODULE crash_litmus ----------------------------
(***************************************************************************)
(* What the backing filesystem's crash consistency leaves to the caller    *)
(* (step 12.8): Ferrite's litmus tests (MClitmus.tla) whose surprising     *)
(* outcome is reachable without an fsync. dcfs adds no crash outcome       *)
(* (CrashRefines), and it adds no atomicity or durability either; each     *)
(* configuration here expects the counterexample:                          *)
(*                                                                         *)
(*  - litmus_replace_metaprefix, litmus_replace_ext4: atomic replace via   *)
(*    rename (ARVR). Create t, write it, rename it over f, with no fsync   *)
(*    before the rename: a power loss may keep the rename and lose t's     *)
(*    data, and dcfs then serves f empty, as the backing filesystem has    *)
(*    it (LitmusOK). The specifications allow it (DFSCQ's metadata-prefix, *)
(*    Ferrite's Definition 7, which the paper relates to the "ext4 data    *)
(*    loss" of 2009); whether ext4 data=ordered with auto_da_alloc can     *)
(*    still produce it is doubtful. Only the sequential regime forbids it  *)
(*    (MC_litmus_replace_seq.cfg).                                         *)
(*  - litmus_create_metaprefix, litmus_create_ext4: atomic create via      *)
(*    rename (ACVR): the same, f new.                                      *)
(*  The outcomes an fsync leaves possible are not limitations of dcfs but  *)
(*  of the backing filesystem, which dcfs passes the fsync to and adds no  *)
(*  barrier to: no_stronger/*.cfg pins them (MClitmus.tla).                *)
(***************************************************************************)
EXTENDS MClitmus
=============================================================================
